    "lawCode": "Amd §§1676 & 1679-b, Pub Auth L; amd §§3 & 5-b, NYS Med Care Fac Fin Ag Act",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S8339\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to the powers\nand duties of the dormitory authority; and  to amend the New York state\nmedical care facilities finance agency act, in relation to equipment\nloans for intellectual property\n\nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nTo address a technical deficiency within the law that authorizes the\nDormitory Authority of the State of New York (DASNY) through its Tax\nExempt Leasing Program (TELP) to finance the lease acquisition of\ncertain equipment. The new provision would explicitly include health\ninformation technology equipment, software, intellectual property, other\nintangible property and ancillary costs associated with that equipment.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\n§ 1. Amends Subdivision 2 of section 1676 of the public authorities law\nby adding a new paragraph (h) to include equipment, intellectual proper-\nty or other intangible property, including information technology and\nsoftware, making those items eligible for tax-exempt financing under the\nUnited States internal revenue code.\n\n§2. Amends Subdivision 18 of section 7413 of the unconsolidated laws to\ndefine \"Equipment loan\" s to be consistent with the provisions outlined\nin section 1 of the bill.\n\n§3. Amends Subdivision 1 of section 74l5-b of the unconsolidated laws to\nbe consistent with the changes made in sections 1 and 2 of the legis-\nlation with respect to equipment loans.\n\n§4. Amends Subdivision 1 of section l679-b of the public authorities law\nto be consistent with the changes made in sections 1 and 2 of the legis-\nlation.\n\n§5. Sets forth an immediate effective date for the legislation.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nThe Dormitory Authority of the State of New York (DASNY) through its Tax\nExempt Leasing Program (TELP) currently is authorized to provide financ-\ning for equipment loans. However, the ability to finance ancillary\nacquisitions and other intangible property is Not explicitly included in\nthat authorization.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is new legislation.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Dormitory Authority currently operates a significant Tax Exempt\nLeasing Program (more than $250 million for fiscal year 2009/2010) that\nfinances the procurement of equipment for numerous institutions such as\nnot for profit hospitals, universities, colleges, and other DASNY not-\nfor-profit clients. As institutions in New York State, especially the\nhealthcare industry, move toward a more efficient record-keeping and\nrecord-sharing, it is important that they be equipped with the tools\nneeded to achieve that goal. Under current law, the Dormitory Authority\nof the State of New York (\"DASNY\"), through its Tax-Exempt Leasing\nProgram (\"Program\"), provides the industry with one such tool. The TELP\nprogram converts commercial equipment leases to tax-exempt equipment\nleases. It allows DASNY to confer tax-exempt status to the equipment\nlease and thereby reduce the borrowing cost for such assets and equip-\nment on behalf of not- for-profit organizations. All funds are provided\nby third party lenders, ie. private financial institutions.\n\nThis bill seeks to enhance Program services by specifying certain addi-\ntional allowable activities including lease financing of electronic\nmedical records (\"EMR\") projects, and the associated activities neces-\nsary for their implementation and development. These activities would\ninclude but not be limited to stand-alone software and hardware,\nconsultant costs, licensure, implementation, training and other capital\ncosts that are necessary to make such projects and equipment/technology\nfunctional, operable and serviceable.\n\nThis bill would assist DASNY's not-for-profit clients, primarily health-\ncare institutions, to invest in technology equipment and software there-\nby preserving jobs, and maintaining current technology and related\nproficiency state-wide. Further, EMR technology is often an extraor-\ndinarily significant fiscal item, exceeding $50 or $60 million in many\ninstances.  Healthcare facilities are finding these projects difficult\nto complete without funding assistance and have sought such assistance\nfrom DASNY in the form of tax-exempt leasing under the TELP program.\n\nThis emerging need, to reduce the financing costs of these acquisitions,\ncan be addressed by clarifying the current TELP statute. This legis-\nlation would allow health care institutions to access funding for the\nprocurement of health information technology equipment software and\nassociated ancillary implementation and licensure costs connected to the\nequipment.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNo cost to the State. Failure to adopt these provisions could result in\nthe loss of approximately $250 million in equipment projects over the\nnext few months.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
